window.INFO={"id":"gold","title":"Queensland Gold Rushes, 1858 to 1877","subtitle":"An information bank about Canoona, Gympie, Charters Towers and the Palmer River","description":"A non-fiction information bank about the Queensland gold rushes at Canoona, Gympie, Charters Towers and the Palmer River: overview, timeline, places, people, real lives and sources. Includes Chinese miners and Aboriginal people.","dateLeft":"Queensland · 1858 to 1877","dateRight":"By Robin McKean","theme":"#231a10","footnote":"Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this resource names people who have died. Everything in it comes from the sources listed on the Sources page.","footer":"Queensland Gold Rushes. A non-fiction information bank by Robin McKean. This resource has no tracking and no cookies. An earlier choose-your-path story version is kept in the archive folder of the project on GitHub.","warning":["This resource is about gold rushes on the Country of Aboriginal people. It says that violence against Aboriginal people and racism against Chinese people happened. It does not describe the violence in detail.","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this resource names people who have died, and that the old records it draws on were written by Europeans."],"review":"Review status: this resource has not yet been reviewed by Aboriginal community members. Review is being sought. Please tell the author if anything needs to change.","sourcesIntro":"Every fact in this resource comes from one of these sources. Newspapers named in the sources can be read on Trove. Queensland State Archives holds the original government records. The Folklore and legends page also uses some sources of this kind. They are listed here with the others, and the stories are labelled as unproven.","imageNote":"All images are old photographs that are in the public domain, and each is credited. No images in this resource were made by artificial intelligence. There are no images of Aboriginal people.","overview":{"image":"gympie","lead":"Gold was found in Queensland many times between 1858 and 1877. Each time, thousands of people travelled to the new field. Each field was on Country where Aboriginal people already lived. Many of the diggers were British or Irish. Many were Chinese.","glance":[{"n":"About 15,000","label":"people at Canoona between September and December 1858","src":["qpCanoona"]},{"n":"About 16,000","label":"diggers on the Gympie field at the height of the rush","src":["qsaGympie"]},{"n":"17,000 and 1,400","label":"Chinese and European people on the Palmer River field by 1876","src":["kirk93"]},{"n":"About 100 tonnes","label":"of gold from the Palmer River field in all","src":["abs150"]}],"sections":[{"h":"Queensland and the gold rushes","p":["At the time of the first rush Queensland was still part of New South Wales. It became a separate colony on 10 December 1859. Early in 1866 the new colony was in a recession made worse by a long drought. A bank had closed and unemployed men were protesting in the streets of Brisbane. The government offered a £3,000 reward for a new goldfield.","The four main rushes were at Canoona near Rockhampton in 1858, Gympie in 1867, Charters Towers in 1871 and 1872, and the Palmer River in 1873 to 1877."],"src":["abs150","qsaGympie"]},{"h":"The rushes in turn","p":["Canoona, 1858. A newspaper reported gold at Canoona on 28 July 1858. An official wrote that the ground taken up by the first diggers could give work to only a very small number of men. Queensland Places says the field was not a fraud, but it could not support anything like 15,000 people. Most of the thousands who came were returned south by the governments in Sydney and Melbourne. A few hundred stayed, and Rockhampton was proclaimed a town on 25 October 1858.","Gympie, 1867. James Nash found gold near the Mary River in August 1867. The Queensland State Archives says the surface gold was mined out within a year, so the miners went underground. The field was worked for about 60 years, and Gympie became known as the town that saved Queensland, because its gold helped end the recession.","Charters Towers, 1871 and 1872. A party of prospectors found gold in December 1871. By the end of September 1872 four crushing mills were working. Charters Towers became Queensland’s premier goldfield and for a time its second largest city.","Palmer River, 1873 to 1877. James Venture Mulligan and six companions found payable gold on 29 June 1873, and Cooktown was settled from October 1873 as the port. By 1876 the field was largely left to Chinese miners."],"src":["slqCanoona","qpCanoona","csc","cqu","qsaGympie","ctc","abs150","kirk84","kirk93"]},{"h":"Chinese miners","p":["In the 1871 census there were 3,305 Chinese people in Queensland. Many Chinese men went to the goldfields to earn money for their families. Many of those who came directly from China were sponsored by Chinese merchants and had to repay a debt before they could mine for themselves.","On the Palmer, Chinese miners worked ground that European miners had left. Many Europeans blamed them for high prices. Historian Noreen Kirkman says it would have been clear to most Europeans that Chinese people were the chief suppliers of vegetables and stores on the field. In 1876 and 1877 Queensland passed laws that made Chinese miners pay more and put a tax on each Chinese person arriving by ship."],"src":["kirk93","kirk87","hodes"]},{"h":"Aboriginal people and the goldfields","p":["Every field was on Aboriginal Country. On the Palmer, historian Noreen Kirkman writes that the Aboriginal way of watching and warning strangers did not survive the arrival of the first miners. The rivers were the main source of food for Aboriginal people, and the miners worked in the same rivers for gold. This is a main reason that conflict began. The Queensland Parks service says that game was killed or driven away, and that waterholes that had been clear became muddy.","On 27 September 1873 a group of miners forcibly removed Aboriginal people from a camp near where they were working. The Native Police were sent to protect the diggers and to carry out “dispersals”, the word used for breaking up Aboriginal camps. The numbers of Aboriginal people killed on the Palmer in the 1870s are not known."],"src":["kirk84","parks"]},{"h":"After the rushes","p":["Gympie’s deep mines kept the town going for decades. By 1910 mining at Charters Towers had slowed. Only about 500 new Chinese people arrived in Queensland between mid-1877 and 1881. Chinese communities continued in Queensland towns. In 1897 Queensland passed a law that gave the government wide control over the lives of Aboriginal people, together with later protection laws."],"src":["qsaGympie","abs150","hodes","aph"]}],"words":[["Rush","A sudden movement of many people to a new goldfield."],["Alluvial gold","Gold in creek gravel."],["Reef gold","Gold in rock."],["Warden","An official who ran a goldfield."],["Miner’s right","A licence to mine."],["Native Police","Aboriginal troopers led by European officers, who were used to break up Aboriginal camps."],["Dispersal","The word used in the records for breaking up an Aboriginal camp by force."]],"left":["This resource does not describe violence against Aboriginal people or Chinese people in detail. It says that it happened, with the sources.","It gives no names or customs of Aboriginal peoples beyond what the sources say. The names of Aboriginal people who lived at each field are mostly not recorded in the sources read here.","It does not quote the insulting words about Aboriginal and Chinese people that appear in the records of the 1870s."]},"timeline":[{"date":"28 July 1858","title":"Gold reported at Canoona","text":"The Moreton Bay Courier reported that gold had been found at Canoona. Canoona is about 40 kilometres north-west of Rockhampton, on the Fitzroy River. The land around Rockhampton and the Fitzroy River is the Country of the Darumbal people.","src":["slqCanoona","qpCanoona","darumbal"]},{"date":"September to December 1858","title":"About 15,000 people at Canoona","text":"An estimated 15,000 people were at Canoona. An official wrote that the ground taken up by the first diggers could give work to only a very small number of men. Queensland Places says the field was not a fraud, but it could not support anything like 15,000 people.","flag":"Only the part of the official’s letter that is quoted in a summary was read. The full letter has not been checked.","src":["qpCanoona","csc"]},{"date":"25 October 1858","title":"Rockhampton proclaimed a town","text":"Most of the thousands who came to Canoona were returned south by the governments in Sydney and Melbourne. A few hundred stayed and started businesses. Rockhampton’s first land sales were on 17 and 18 November 1858.","src":["cqu","qpCanoona"]},{"date":"10 December 1859","title":"Queensland becomes a separate colony","text":"Until then Queensland was part of New South Wales.","src":["abs150"]},{"date":"Early 1866","title":"Recession and a reward","text":"Queensland was in a recession made worse by a long drought. A bank had closed and unemployed men were protesting in the streets of Brisbane. The government offered a £3,000 reward for a new goldfield.","src":["qsaGympie"]},{"date":"August to October 1867","title":"James Nash finds gold near Gympie","text":"In August 1867 James Nash found gold near the Mary River. On 16 October 1867 he wrote to claim the reward. The town was first called Nashville and later Gympie, from the Gubbi Gubbi word gimpi gimpi, the name of a stinging tree.","flag":"The Queensland State Archives says gold was noticed near Gympie in 1851 by John Carne Bidwill, so who “discovered” gold at Gympie is not simple.","src":["qsaGympie","grmNash"]},{"date":"1868 to 1871","title":"Miners go underground at Gympie","text":"The Queensland State Archives says the surface gold was mined out within a year, so the miners went underground. At the height of the rush there were about 16,000 diggers on the field.","src":["qsaGympie"]},{"date":"December 1871","title":"Gold found at Charters Towers","text":"A party of prospectors found gold near a hill at what became Charters Towers. The party was Hugh Mosman, George Clarke, a man named Fraser, and an Aboriginal horse boy named Jupiter, who is credited with finding the gold-bearing quartz.","flag":"The third man’s name is written as John Fraser in one source and James Fraser in another. Only “Fraser” is used here.","src":["ctc","monJupiter"]},{"date":"26 January 1872","title":"Claim made at Charters Towers","text":"Hugh Mosman applied for a protection area for the claim. The place was named after the Gold Commissioner, W. S. E. M. Charters.","src":["ctc"]},{"date":"September 1872","title":"Four crushing mills at Charters Towers","text":"By the end of September 1872 four crushing mills were working.","src":["ctc"]},{"date":"1872","title":"The Hann expedition reports gold on the Palmer","text":"The Hann expedition reported gold on the Palmer River before Mulligan’s find in 1873.","src":["kirk84","kirk93","parks"]},{"date":"29 June 1873","title":"Mulligan finds payable gold on the Palmer","text":"James Venture Mulligan and six companions found payable alluvial gold. The rush began in September 1873, and Cooktown was settled from October 1873 as the port.","src":["kirk84","kirk93","parks"]},{"date":"27 September 1873","title":"Aboriginal people removed from a camp on the Palmer","text":"A group of miners forcibly removed Aboriginal people from a camp near where they were working. According to Mulligan, the attack was not provoked, and it marked the start of the conflict. In the first month of the rush the Aboriginal people returned and frightened miners out of the gullies two or three times.","src":["kirk84"]},{"date":"May 1874","title":"Chinese miners head for the Palmer","text":"By late May 1874, Chinese miners and their families were heading to the Palmer from the Etheridge and Charters Towers fields.","src":["kirk93"]},{"date":"August to December 1874","title":"Chinese population on the Palmer grows","text":"By the end of August 1874 there were about a thousand Chinese on the Palmer, with a temple led by Dr King Chong. By the end of 1874 there were about 1,500, almost 40 per cent of the field’s people.","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"date":"May 1875","title":"A notice at Sandy Creek","text":"A notice at Sandy Creek threatened to hang any Chinese man found higher up the creek. Kirkman says it was a sign of anger, not a plan. The large riots that had taken place at Lambing Flat in New South Wales in 1860 and 1861 did not happen on the Palmer.","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"date":"1875","title":"First millionaire miner at Charters Towers","text":"Frank Stubley became the first millionaire miner on the field.","src":["ctc"]},{"date":"July and October 1875","title":"Thousands of Chinese miners; gold sent to Hong Kong","text":"In 1875 steamers brought thousands more from China. About 9,000 Chinese were on the Palmer in July 1875. In October 1875 two steamers carried 14,000 ounces of gold to Hong Kong, including an 80-ounce nugget.","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"date":"March 1876","title":"The Hodgkinson rush begins","text":"After the Hodgkinson rush began, the Palmer was largely left to Chinese miners. Warden Sellheim had to punish a few Europeans who tried to jump Chinese claims. By 1876 there were about 17,000 Chinese and 1,400 Europeans on the field.","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"date":"1876","title":"Queensland passes a bill on Chinese miners","text":"The Queensland parliament passed a bill that made Chinese and other Asian miners pay a miner’s right of £3, six times the fee for European miners, and business licences of £10. The governor sent the bill to London before approving it. It became law in 1877.","src":["kirk93","kirk87","abs150","absYB"]},{"date":"January to May 1877","title":"7,708 Chinese miners arrive","text":"A total of 7,708 Chinese miners arrived on the field in the first five months of 1877, taking the population to about 18,000. By the end of May the average earnings were estimated at only three pennyweights.","src":["kirk87"]},{"date":"1877","title":"A £10 tax on Chinese arrivals","text":"Queensland passed a law that put a £10 tax on each Chinese person arriving by ship, allowed one Chinese passenger for every 10 tons of a ship’s size, and refunded the tax to anyone who left within three years without breaking the law or costing the colony money. A later law banned Asian and African miners from new goldfields for three years after a discovery.","flag":"Sources give the date of the later law as 1877 or 1878.","src":["hodes","abs150","absYB","kirk87"]},{"date":"1877","title":"Aboriginal people on the Palmer half starved","text":"The Queenslander reported that Native Police officers said most Aboriginal people on the Palmer were half starved, because the Europeans had taken the watercourses. The paper said that Aboriginal people killed horses and cattle out of necessity.","src":["kirk84"]},{"date":"From 1877 to 1880","title":"Fewer Native Police","text":"From 1877 the numbers of Native Police were reduced across the colony. By 1880 the number was less than half that of 1876. Kirkman writes that by the end of the decade the catastrophic disruption of Aboriginal society and the decline in the numbers of Europeans and Chinese had reduced the level of conflict.","src":["kirk84"]},{"date":"1877 to 1888","title":"Later laws on Chinese immigration","text":"Only about 500 new Chinese people arrived in the colony between mid-1877 and 1881. In 1884 Queensland raised the tax to £30. In 1888, after a conference of the colonies, it passed a new law like those in the southern colonies.","src":["hodes","absYB"]},{"date":"1894 and 1898","title":"Measles, then removal of Aboriginal people from Gympie","text":"A local history society says a measles outbreak in 1894 struck the Aboriginal camp at Gympie, and that in 1898 the surviving Aboriginal people from Gympie were removed to the Bogimbah Creek reserve on Fraser Island.","flag":"This comes from one local history society article. It should be checked with Kabi Kabi custodians before it is repeated.","src":["gfhsAb"]},{"date":"1897","title":"A law controlling Aboriginal workers","text":"Queensland passed the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act. Under it, an Aboriginal worker could be employed only under a permit from a protector, and employers had to sign an agreement. Together with later protection laws, it gave the government wide control over the lives of Aboriginal people.","src":["aph"]},{"date":"1900 to 1910","title":"Highest production at Gympie; Charters Towers slows","text":"Gympie’s gold production was highest between 1900 and 1910. By 1910 mining at Charters Towers had slowed.","src":["qsaGympie","abs150"]}],"places":[{"name":"Canoona","where":"About 40 kilometres north-west of Rockhampton, on the Fitzroy River · Darumbal Country","text":["Canoona was the site of Queensland’s first big rush, in 1858. An estimated 15,000 people were there between September and December 1858. The field was not a fraud, but it could not support that many people.","The Darumbal people are the Traditional Owners of the Rockhampton and Capricorn Coast area, including the Fitzroy River catchment. No source was found that describes what happened to Darumbal people at Canoona in 1858."],"src":["slqCanoona","qpCanoona","darumbal"]},{"name":"Rockhampton","where":"Fitzroy River · Darumbal Country","text":["Rockhampton was proclaimed a town on 25 October 1858, and its first land sales were on 17 and 18 November 1858. A few hundred of the people who came to Canoona stayed and started businesses."],"src":["cqu"]},{"name":"Gympie","where":"Mary River · Kabi Kabi (Gubbi Gubbi) Country","image":"gympie","text":["The rush began after James Nash found gold in August 1867. The town was first called Nashville and later Gympie. The photograph above shows Mary Street in 1868, with Tattersall’s, the saleyards, the Bank of New South Wales and the Golden Age Hotel.","A local history society says that an Aboriginal camp formed at the edge of the town in the late 1860s, and that Aboriginal people worked as guides, carriers and timber workers. It also says they were often paid in rations or goods, not wages. Library records name more than 260 Chinese people on the goldfield, and say they were later gardeners, shopkeepers and miners. The same records say Europeans harassed Chinese people but that the courts did not accept it. Floods sometimes destroyed the Chinese gardens."],"src":["qsaGympie","grmNash","gfhsAb","grmChinese","gfhsChinese"]},{"name":"Charters Towers","where":"North Queensland · Country of the Gudjal people and others","image":"towers","text":["Gold was found here in December 1871. Four crushing mills were working by the end of September 1872. The Bank of New South Wales branch (photographed about 1875) opened in 1872. Charters Towers became Queensland’s premier goldfield and for a time its second largest city.","The regional council says the area is the Country of the Gudjal people and others."],"src":["ctc","abs150"]},{"name":"The Palmer River","where":"North Queensland","text":["Gold was reported by the Hann expedition in 1872, and Mulligan and six companions found payable alluvial gold on 29 June 1873. The Palmer gave about 100 tonnes of gold in all. The field was unusual: by 1876 there were about 17,000 Chinese and 1,400 Europeans.","The rivers were the main source of food for Aboriginal people, and the miners worked in the same rivers for gold. The people of the Palmer River country are the Traditional Owners; this resource does not name them because no source read here does."],"src":["kirk84","kirk93","abs150","parks"]},{"name":"Cooktown","where":"North Queensland coast","image":"cooktown","text":["Cooktown was settled from October 1873 as the port for the Palmer River goldfield. The photograph above shows Charlotte Street about 1874.","In October 1875 two steamers carried 14,000 ounces of gold from the Palmer to Hong Kong, including an 80-ounce nugget."],"src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"name":"Sandy Creek and the Hodgkinson","where":"North Queensland","text":["In May 1875 a notice at Sandy Creek threatened to hang any Chinese man found higher up the creek. Kirkman says it was a sign of anger, not a plan. After the Hodgkinson rush began in March 1876, the Palmer was largely left to Chinese miners."],"src":["kirk93","kirk87"]}],"people":[{"kind":"person","name":"James Nash","dates":"Gympie, 1867","text":"Found gold near the Mary River in August 1867 and wrote on 16 October 1867 to claim the £3,000 reward.","src":["qsaGympie","grmNash"]},{"kind":"person","name":"John Carne Bidwill","dates":"Gympie, 1851","text":"The Queensland State Archives says gold was noticed near Gympie in 1851 by Bidwill.","src":["qsaGympie"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Hugh Mosman","dates":"Charters Towers, 1871 and 1872","text":"Part of the prospecting party of December 1871 and applied for a protection area for the claim on 26 January 1872.","src":["ctc"]},{"kind":"person","name":"George Clarke and Fraser","dates":"Charters Towers, 1871","text":"The other two European members of the party of December 1871. The third man’s name is written as John Fraser in one source and James Fraser in another.","src":["ctc","monJupiter"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Jupiter Mosman","dates":"Charters Towers, 1871","text":"An Aboriginal horse boy, credited with finding the gold-bearing quartz. His own name and his people are not known. See Real lives.","src":["monJupiter","slqJupiter","ctc"]},{"kind":"person","name":"W. S. E. M. Charters","text":"The Gold Commissioner after whom Charters Towers was named.","src":["ctc"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Frank Stubley","dates":"1875","text":"The first millionaire miner at Charters Towers.","src":["ctc"]},{"kind":"person","name":"James Venture Mulligan","dates":"Palmer River, 1873","text":"Found payable alluvial gold on the Palmer on 29 June 1873 with six companions.","src":["kirk84","parks"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Dr King Chong","dates":"Palmer River, 1874","text":"The leader of a temple on the Palmer in August 1874.","src":["kirk93"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Warden Sellheim","dates":"Palmer River, 1876","text":"Had to punish a few Europeans who tried to jump Chinese claims.","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"kind":"person","name":"Taam Sze Pui","dates":"Palmer River, 1877","text":"A Chinese man who arrived on the Palmer in 1877 and wrote about the field. See Real lives.","src":["kirk87"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Chinese miners and merchants","text":"Thousands of Chinese people worked the goldfields. Many came to earn money for their families. Many of those who came directly from China were sponsored by Chinese merchants and had to repay a debt before they could mine for themselves.","image":"digger","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Darumbal people","text":"Traditional Owners of the Rockhampton and Capricorn Coast area, including the Fitzroy River catchment, where Canoona is.","src":["darumbal"]},{"kind":"group","name":"Kabi Kabi (Gubbi Gubbi) people","text":"The Aboriginal people of the Gympie area. The name Gympie comes from the Gubbi Gubbi word gimpi gimpi.","src":["qsaGympie","gfhsAb"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Gudjal people and others","text":"Named by the regional council as the people of the Charters Towers area.","src":["ctc"]},{"kind":"group","name":"The Native Police","text":"Detachments of Aboriginal troopers led by European officers. They were sent to protect the diggers and to carry out “dispersals”. Kirkman says little is known about the way the force worked.","src":["kirk84"]},{"kind":"org","name":"The Hann expedition","dates":"1872","text":"Reported gold on the Palmer River in 1872.","src":["kirk84","kirk93"]},{"kind":"org","name":"The Queensland parliament","text":"Passed the laws of 1876 and 1877 on Chinese miners, and the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act in 1897.","src":["kirk87","hodes","aph"]}],"lives":[{"name":"Jupiter Mosman","dates":"Charters Towers, December 1871","text":["Jupiter was an Aboriginal horse boy in the party of prospectors who found gold at Charters Towers in December 1871. He is credited with finding the gold-bearing quartz. “Jupiter” is the name recorded for him. His own name and his people are not known.","A book about him says that it took 30 years for his find to be acknowledged and a further 50 years for a monument to be put up. This resource does not say what Jupiter thought or felt, because no source records it."],"src":["monJupiter","slqJupiter","ctc"]},{"name":"James Nash","dates":"Gympie, 1867","text":["James Nash found gold near the Mary River in August 1867, at a time when the colony was in a recession. On 16 October 1867 he wrote to claim the government’s £3,000 reward. His letter is held by the Queensland State Archives. The town was first called Nashville."],"src":["qsaGympie","grmNash"]},{"name":"James Venture Mulligan","dates":"Palmer River, 1873","text":["Mulligan and six companions found payable alluvial gold on the Palmer on 29 June 1873. According to Mulligan, the attack of 27 September 1873, when miners forcibly removed Aboriginal people from a camp, was not provoked, and it marked the start of the conflict."],"src":["kirk84","parks"]},{"name":"Taam Sze Pui","dates":"Palmer River, 1877","text":["Taam Sze Pui arrived on the Palmer in 1877, in the same year that 7,708 Chinese miners arrived on the field in five months. He wrote that many of the Chinese miners who had arrived earlier were poor and ill and were thinking of leaving."],"flag":"Only a summary of what he wrote was read, so his words are not quoted.","src":["kirk87"]},{"name":"Dr King Chong and the Chinese community on the Palmer","dates":"1874","text":["By the end of August 1874 there were about a thousand Chinese on the Palmer, with a temple led by Dr King Chong. By the end of 1874 there were about 1,500 Chinese, almost 40 per cent of the field’s people."],"src":["kirk93","kirk87"]}],"know":[{"t":"Gympie was called “the town that saved Queensland”, because its gold helped end the recession of the 1860s.","src":["qsaGympie"]},{"t":"Gympie is named after the Gubbi Gubbi word gimpi gimpi, the name of a stinging tree.","src":["qsaGympie"]},{"t":"Jupiter’s part in finding gold at Charters Towers took about 30 years to be acknowledged, and a monument was put up a further 50 years later.","src":["slqJupiter"]},{"t":"Charters Towers became Queensland’s premier goldfield and for a time its second largest city.","src":["abs150","ctc"]},{"t":"The Palmer River field gave about 100 tonnes of gold in all.","src":["abs150"]},{"t":"In October 1875 two steamers carried 14,000 ounces of gold to Hong Kong, including an 80-ounce nugget.","src":["kirk93"]},{"t":"By 1876 there were about 17,000 Chinese and 1,400 Europeans on the Palmer River field.","src":["kirk93"]},{"t":"The large riots that took place at Lambing Flat in New South Wales in 1860 and 1861 did not happen on the Palmer.","src":["kirk93","kirk87"]},{"t":"Chinese miners and Chinese merchants were the chief suppliers of vegetables and stores on the Palmer, according to historian Noreen Kirkman.","src":["kirk93"]},{"t":"In 1877 the average earnings on the Palmer were estimated at only three pennyweights.","src":["kirk87"]},{"t":"Canoona was not a fraud, but it could not support anything like 15,000 people.","src":["qpCanoona"]}],"disagree":["Who “discovered” gold is not simple. The Queensland State Archives says gold was noticed near Gympie in 1851 by John Carne Bidwill. At the Palmer, the Hann expedition reported gold in 1872 before Mulligan’s find in 1873. At Charters Towers, Jupiter is credited with the find but was not credited for about 30 years.","The name of the third man in the Charters Towers party is written as John Fraser in one source and James Fraser in another. Only “Fraser” is used here.","Sources give the date of the law banning Asian and African miners from new goldfields as 1877 or 1878.","The numbers of Aboriginal people killed on the Palmer in the 1870s are not known. Kirkman writes that little is known about how the Native Police worked.","No source was found that describes what happened to Darumbal people at Canoona in 1858.","Some information about Aboriginal people at Gympie, including the 1894 measles outbreak and the 1898 removals, comes from a local history society article. It needs to be checked by Kabi Kabi custodians.","Only the part of the 1858 Colonial Secretary letter that is quoted in a summary was read. The full letter has not been checked.","The Queensland Parks page on the Palmer goldfield could not be opened directly. Its text was read through a search summary and should be checked on the page.","Almost all the records of Aboriginal people and Chinese miners on the Palmer were written by Europeans. Kirkman says the evidence is almost entirely European in origin, and is often hostile or at best indifferent. Chinese miners’ own accounts are rare.","The Folklore and legends page contains stories that are not proven. Each is labelled “Folklore: unproven” and says what is documented and what is doubtful. These stories are not used as facts anywhere else in this resource."],"care":["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this resource names people who have died, including Jupiter Mosman and James Nash, and that it says violence against Aboriginal people happened.","The records of the 1870s often used insulting words about Aboriginal and Chinese people. They are not quoted here. This resource uses the words “Aboriginal people”, “Chinese miners” and “Native Police”.","The author wants to ask Traditional Owners and Aboriginal community members to review this resource before it is used widely. The people to ask include the Darumbal people for Canoona, Kabi Kabi (Gubbi Gubbi) custodians for Gympie, Gudjal and other Traditional Owner groups for Charters Towers, and Traditional Owners of the Palmer River country. Until that review is done, please treat this resource as a draft."],"readMore":["qsaSearch","trove"],"images":{"gympie":{"file":"images/mary-street-gympie-1868.jpg","w":960,"h":622,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a wide dirt street with wooden buildings and groups of men standing in front of them.","caption":"Mary Street, Gympie, 1868. The buildings include Tattersall’s, the saleyards, the Bank of New South Wales and the Golden Age Hotel.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_15578_Mary_Street,_Gympie,_1868.jpg"},"cooktown":{"file":"images/charlotte-street-cooktown-1874.jpg","w":960,"h":616,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a street with a few wooden buildings and tents.","caption":"Charlotte Street, Cooktown, about 1874. Cooktown was the port for the Palmer River goldfield.","credit":"Unknown photographer, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte_Street,_Cooktown.jpg"},"towers":{"file":"images/charters-towers-bank-1875.jpg","w":960,"h":564,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a small wooden bank building with a sign, with a horse and cart in front.","caption":"The Bank of New South Wales at Charters Towers, about 1875. The branch opened in 1872.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_45675_Charters_Towers_branch_of_the_Bank_of_New_South_Wales,_ca._1875.jpg"},"digger":{"file":"images/chinese-gold-digger-1860s.jpg","w":817,"h":1000,"tall":true,"alt":"A black and white photograph of a man in a wide hat with a long pole across his shoulders. A wooden box hangs from one end and a bucket from the other.","caption":"A Chinese gold digger with his mining tools hung from a pole, in the 1860s. Photograph by Richard Daintree.","credit":"Richard Daintree, copied by State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_116084_Chinese_gold_digger_starting_for_work,_ca._1860s.jpg"}},"teacherNotes":["Before you use this in class, please seek advice from Traditional Owners of your area. This resource has not yet been reviewed by Aboriginal community members.","Give students the content warning before they start. Tell them they may stop at any time. Check whether any students have family connections to the events.","Suggested use: students read the Overview and Timeline, choose one rush to study on the Places and Real lives pages, then answer two or three discussion questions.","The answers are suggestions. They are behind “Show” buttons. The student version has no answers in its code.","Some of the linked source pages quote offensive words from the records. Look at them before you show them to a class."],"discuss":[{"q":"Why did thousands of people go to Canoona in 1858, and why did most of them leave?","a":["A newspaper said gold had been found. The ground taken up by the first diggers could give work to only a very small number of men, and the field could not support about 15,000 people. The governments in Sydney and Melbourne returned most people south. A few hundred stayed, and Rockhampton became a town.","Look for: the figure of 15,000, the date of Rockhampton’s proclamation (25 October 1858), and the point that the field was not a fraud."]},{"q":"Why did the Queensland government offer a reward for gold in 1866, and why was Gympie called “the town that saved Queensland”?","a":["Queensland was in a recession. A bank had closed, and unemployed men were protesting in Brisbane. The reward was £3,000. The Gympie field brought new people, money and jobs, and its gold helped end the recession."]},{"q":"Chinese miners worked ground that European miners had left. What did European miners say about them, and what do the records show?","a":["Chinese miners worked ground left when Europeans rushed to new finds. Many Europeans blamed them for high prices. The records show that Chinese people were the chief suppliers of vegetables and stores on the Palmer.","Good answers name at least two claims and test them against evidence."]},{"q":"List three ways that the gold rushes changed life for Aboriginal people in the places in this resource. Which source do you use for each?","a":["Examples: the loss of waterholes and food sources on the Palmer (Kirkman; Queensland Parks); forced removal from a camp on 27 September 1873 (Kirkman); Aboriginal workers at Gympie being paid in rations or goods (local history society, to be checked with Kabi Kabi custodians); the Aboriginal camp at Gympie being struck by measles in 1894 and the people removed in 1898 (same source).","Mark any answer that goes beyond the sources, so students learn to say what a source does not say."]},{"q":"Noreen Kirkman writes that the evidence about Aboriginal people on the Palmer is almost entirely European in origin. How does this change what we can know? Which voices are missing?","a":["The records show European views, so they may miss Aboriginal views and may describe Aboriginal people unfairly. Missing voices include Aboriginal people and many Chinese miners, whose own accounts are rare.","Ask what other kinds of evidence might help: oral history, Traditional Owner knowledge, archaeology."]},{"q":"By 1876 there were about 17,000 Chinese and 1,400 Europeans on the Palmer. Why did the colony’s government make new laws in 1876 and 1877? Were they fair?","a":["The government and many Europeans feared and resented the number of Chinese miners. The laws made Chinese miners pay more and put a tax of £10 on each arrival.","Students should give a reasoned opinion and refer to the facts. A good answer notes that the Palmer law was passed in response to a field where Europeans were a minority."]},{"q":"Jupiter, the Aboriginal horse boy, is credited with finding gold at Charters Towers, but it took about 30 years for this to be acknowledged. Why might that be? What does it mean that his own name is not known?","a":["The sources do not say why. Students might suggest that records were kept by the prospectors, and that Aboriginal people were not usually credited.","The answer should avoid claiming to know what Jupiter thought."]},{"q":"Write a paragraph for or against this statement: “The £10 tax on Chinese arrivals in 1877 was a fair way to control immigration.” Use two facts.","a":["Students should use facts such as the amount of the tax, who paid it, the refund rule and the numbers who arrived in 1877. A good answer deals with the opposite view."]},{"q":"What would you want to ask Traditional Owners before using this resource with a class? List three questions.","a":["Examples: Who are the right people to speak about this Country? Which parts are wrong, or hurt? Is there a story or a name that should be told by them and not by the author? Is it all right to name people who have died?"]}],"sources":{"absYB":{"t":"Australian Bureau of Statistics. Year Book Australia, 1925: “Chinese in Australia”.","u":"https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/featurearticlesbytitle/4A6A63F3D85F7770CA2569DE00200137?OpenDocument=","short":"ABS Year Book 1925","note":"Queensland’s 1876 gold fields amendment, its approval in 1877, and the later changes of 1884 and 1888."},"abs150":{"t":"Australian Bureau of Statistics. “150 Years of Queensland’s Economic History: Key Dates, Facts and Figures”. Qld Stats, February 2009.","u":"https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/Previousproducts/1318.3Feature%20Article14Feb%202009?issue=Feb+2009&num=&opendocument=&prodno=1318.3&tabname=Summary&view=","short":"ABS, 150 years of Queensland","note":"Separation on 10 December 1859, the size of the Gympie, Palmer and Charters Towers rushes, and the 1877 laws."},"ctc":{"t":"Charters Towers Regional Council. “History of our Region”.","u":"https://www.charterstowers.qld.gov.au/Council/Charters-towers-history/History-of-our-region","short":"Charters Towers Regional Council, history","note":"The discovery in December 1871, the claim of 26 January 1872, the four mills of 1872 and the first millionaire miner in 1875."},"csc":{"t":"Colonial Secretary’s Correspondence, transcribed by State Library of Queensland volunteers (FromThePage).","u":"https://fromthepage.com/slqld/colonial-secretary-s-correspondence/work-33054-013189","short":"Colonial Secretary letter, 1858","note":"An official’s 1858 letter about the Canoona field. Only the part quoted in a summary was read."},"cqu":{"t":"Central Queensland University. “An Overview of Rockhampton’s History” (PDF).","u":"https://library-resources.cqu.edu.au/cqcollection/manuscripts/short-manuscripts/rockhampton.pdf","short":"CQU, Rockhampton history","note":"Dates for the proclamation of Rockhampton, the first land sales, and the return south of most of the people who came."},"darumbal":{"t":"Darumbal Enterprises. Darumbal History.","u":"https://darumbal.com.au/darumbal-culture/darumbal-history/","short":"Darumbal Enterprises"},"gfhsAb":{"t":"Gympie Family History Society. “Aboriginal Life on the Gympie Goldfields” (Jody Allen).","u":"https://www.gfhs.com.au/aboriginal-life-on-the-gympie-goldfields/","short":"Gympie Family History Society, Aboriginal life","note":"A local history society article. It is a secondary source, so Kabi Kabi custodians should check anything taken from it."},"gfhsChinese":{"t":"Gympie Family History Society. “Ah Young: Chinese Immigrant and Mary Street Merchant”.","u":"https://www.gfhs.com.au/ah-young/","short":"Gympie Family History Society, Ah Young","note":"Says Chinese miners worked ground left by European diggers. The article is partly a retelling, so only that general point is used."},"grmChinese":{"t":"Gympie Regional Libraries. “Chinese miners, gardeners and shopkeepers in Gympie”. Gympie Regional Memories.","u":"https://gympieregionalmemories.com/2022/03/11/chinese-miners-gardeners-shopkeepers-in-gympie/","short":"Gympie Regional Memories, Chinese people","note":"A library research project that found more than 260 named Chinese people on the Gympie goldfield."},"grmNash":{"t":"Gympie Regional Libraries. “Gold Discovered in Gympie: James Nash”. Gympie Regional Memories.","u":"https://gympieregionalmemories.com/2022/05/12/gold-discovered-in-gympie/","short":"Gympie Regional Memories, Nash","note":"Shows James Nash’s letter of 16 October 1867 (Queensland State Archives, item 1137542)."},"hodes":{"t":"Jeremy Hodes. “John Douglas and restricting Chinese migration to Queensland in 1877”. Queensland History (blog), 2015.","u":"https://queenslandhistory.blogspot.com/2015/05/john-douglas-and-restricting-chinese.html","short":"Hodes, 1877 law","note":"The £10 tax, the ship limit and the refund rule."},"kirk87":{"t":"Noreen Kirkman. “Chinese Miners on the Palmer”. Journal of the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, vol. 13, no. 2, 1987.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/24925/1/s00855804_1987_13_2_49.pdf","short":"Kirkman, “Chinese Miners on the Palmer” (1987)","note":"Numbers of Chinese miners in 1875 to 1877, the 1876 gold fields bill and the miners’ own accounts."},"kirk93":{"t":"Noreen Kirkman. “From Minority to Majority: Chinese on the Palmer River Gold-field, 1873–1876”. In Henry Reynolds (ed.), Race Relations in North Queensland, James Cook University, 1993.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/26810/1/26810_Kirkman_in_Reynolds_Race_relations_1993_1.pdf","short":"Kirkman, “From Minority to Majority” (1993)","note":"Uses Queensland State Archives files and newspapers such as the Queenslander and the Cooktown Herald."},"kirk84":{"t":"Noreen Kirkman. “The Palmer goldfield, 1873–1883”. Honours thesis, James Cook University, 1984.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/57117/1/57117-kirkman-1984-thesis.pdf","short":"Kirkman, Palmer goldfield (1984)","note":"The most detailed study of the Palmer. It says its evidence about Chinese and Aboriginal people is almost entirely European in origin."},"monJupiter":{"t":"Monument Australia. “Jupiter Mosman”.","u":"https://www.monumentaustralia.org/themes/people/indigenous/display/91079-jupiter-mosman","short":"Monument Australia, Jupiter Mosman","note":"Says Jupiter Mosman’s tribal name is unknown and that he found the gold-bearing quartz in December 1871."},"qsaGympie":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. “Gold at Gympie”. Stories from the Archives, 30 August 2021.","u":"https://blogs.archives.qld.gov.au/2021/08/30/gold-at-gympie/","short":"QSA, Gold at Gympie","note":"The 1866 recession, the £3,000 reward, James Nash’s find, how quickly the surface gold ran out, and the name Gympie."},"parks":{"t":"Queensland Parks and Forests. “Nature, culture and history: Palmer Goldfield Resources Reserve”.","u":"https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/palmer-goldfield/about/culture","short":"Queensland Parks, Palmer Goldfield","note":"The date of Mulligan’s find and the effects on Aboriginal people. The page could not be opened directly, so its text was read through a search summary."},"qpCanoona":{"t":"Queensland Places. “Canoona”. University of Queensland.","u":"https://queenslandplaces.com.au/canoona","short":"Queensland Places, Canoona","note":"Gives the 15,000 estimate and says the field was not a “duffer” but could not support that many people."},"qsaSearch":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. Archives Search (online catalogue).","u":"https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/","short":"Queensland State Archives, Archives Search","note":"To read more: search for gold fields warden records, mining warden court records and Colonial Secretary letters."},"aph":{"t":"Parliament of Australia, Senate Legal and Constitutional Affairs References Committee. “Unfinished business: Indigenous stolen wages”, Chapter 2, “Life under the protection acts”. 2006.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/parliamentary_business/committees/senate/legal_and_constitutional_affairs/completed_inquiries/2004-07/stolen_wages/report/c02","short":"Senate report on stolen wages, chapter 2","note":"Describes the Queensland Act of 1897, the permit and agreement rules for Aboriginal workers, and the control of wages."},"slqCanoona":{"t":"State Library of Queensland. “Gold Fever – The Canoona Delusion near Rockhampton” (blog).","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/gold-fever-canoona-delusion-near-rockhampton","short":"SLQ blog on Canoona","note":"Gives the location of Canoona and quotes the Moreton Bay Courier of 28 July 1858, page 2 (on Trove)."},"slqJupiter":{"t":"State Library of Queensland Shop. “Jupiter Mosman: The Gold Discoverer”.","u":"https://shop.slq.qld.gov.au/products/9781922643216","short":"SLQ, Jupiter Mosman book","note":"Says it took 30 years for his find to be acknowledged and a further 50 years for a monument."},"trove":{"t":"National Library of Australia. Trove: digitised newspapers.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/","short":"Trove","note":"To read more: search for the Moreton Bay Courier (1858), the Nashville Times (1868), the Queenslander and the Cooktown Herald (1874 to 1877)."},"gfhsDenman":{"t":"Gympie Family History Society. “Who was the first discoverer of gold in Gympie”. Website article.","u":"https://www.gfhs.com.au/who-was-the-first-discoverer-of-gold-in-gympie/","short":"Gympie Family History Society, Denman","note":"Reprints R. J. Denman’s letter of December 1869 to the Maryborough Mail and a Maryborough Chronicle report of December 1868 that says Denman was the first to tell Nash the place."},"nash1917":{"t":"James Nash. “How I discovered Gympie”. The Queenslander, 13 October 1917. Nash wrote it for the Gympie Times in 1896.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/22343345","short":"Nash, “How I discovered Gympie”","note":"Trove could not be opened directly. Its content was read as quoted at length by a research website, CQ Diggings."},"fergDugdale":{"t":"John E. Ferguson and Jim S. Dugdale. “The Nugget from Nashville (Gympie) 1868: Of Perseverance and a Prince”. Journal of Australasian Mining History, vol. 18, October 2020, pp. 49–64.","u":"https://www.mininghistory.asn.au/wp-content/uploads/3.-Ferguson-Vol-18-com.pdf","short":"Ferguson and Dugdale, the Curtis nugget (2020)","note":"Who found the nugget, how it was carried, its weight, the 804 ounce error and the search for a photograph with Prince Alfred."},"aflacaMW":{"t":"“Mt Wheeler Nugget’s”. A compilation of newspaper articles from 1869 to 1931, aflaca.org.au.","u":"https://aflaca.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/Mt-Wheeler-Nuggets.pdf","short":"Mt Wheeler Nuggets compilation","note":"Gives the full text of the Trove articles used for the Mount Wheeler story."},"troveMW1869":{"t":"“Important Gold Discoveries!” Rockhampton Bulletin and Central Queensland Advertiser, 17 July 1869, p. 2.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/51576185","short":"Rockhampton Bulletin, 17 July 1869","note":"Read in the aflaca.org.au compilation."},"troveMW26":{"t":"“The Mount Wheeler Diggings”. The Brisbane Courier, 26 July 1869, p. 3.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/1291715","short":"Brisbane Courier, 26 July 1869","note":"Read in the aflaca.org.au compilation."},"troveMW1924":{"t":"“On the Track”. Morning Bulletin (Rockhampton), 12 May 1924, p. 12.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/54273624","short":"Morning Bulletin, 12 May 1924","note":"Read in the aflaca.org.au compilation. It records one theory that this resource does not repeat because it stereotypes Aboriginal people."},"hillPalmer":{"t":"William Hill. “The Palmer Goldfield: Early Day Experiences”. Reprinted on the Chapel Hill family history website.","u":"https://www.chapelhill.homeip.net/FamilyHistory/Other/QueenslandHistory/ThePalmerRiverGoldfieldEarlyDayExperiences.htm","short":"Hill, Palmer goldfield early day experiences","note":"The page contains insulting words about Chinese people from 1907. Those words are not copied here."}},"folklore":{"intro":"Many yarns were told about gold rushes. Prospectors, newspapers and later writers told and retold them. This page collects five that can be found in published sources. For each one, read the story first. Then read what we actually know.","banner":{"head":"Stories people told, not proven history","text":["Read this first: the stories on this page are folklore. They are not proven history.","These are yarns, legends and tall tales that people have told about this period. Each one really exists in a published source, and the source is linked. But the stories themselves are largely unproven. Some may be partly true. Some are probably wrong.","Each story has a “What we actually know” part. It says what is documented and why the rest is doubtful. Do not use anything on this page as a fact in an essay or a test.","This page does not include Aboriginal Dreaming stories or any sacred or restricted stories."]},"stories":[{"title":"Jupiter and the runaway horses","where":"Charters Towers · December 1871","text":["One telling of the discovery says a fierce thunderstorm scattered the horses of a party of prospectors. Next morning, while the party searched for the horses, the first gold at Charters Towers was found.","In this telling the party was Hugh Mosman, George Clarke, John Fraser and the Aboriginal horse boy Jupiter. The gold was found at about the end of 1871."],"from":"The Charters Towers Regional Council history page tells the storm story in its “Brief History”. The same page quotes Hugh Mosman and Jupiter describing the find in their own words.","known":["What is documented: A party of prospectors found gold near Charters Towers at the end of 1871. Hugh Mosman applied for a protection area at Ravenswood on 26 January 1872. Jupiter, an Aboriginal horse boy, is credited with finding the gold-bearing quartz.","Why the rest is doubtful: The council page quotes Jupiter. He says he found a nice piece of stone while he was out from the camp, took it back to Hugh Mosman, and they dug it out with a pick. He does not mention a storm or runaway horses. The council page also quotes Hugh Mosman describing masses of quartz lying on the surface. He does not mention a storm either. The page does not say who first told the storm story or when. The storm and the horses are unproven."],"src":["ctc","monJupiter"]},{"title":"Who told James Nash where to dig?","where":"Gympie · August to September 1867","text":["James Nash is credited with finding the Gympie goldfield in 1867. A second man, a timber getter named R. J. Denman, said that he told Nash the right place to look. In this story Denman gave Nash directions to the creek where the gold was found, and Nash found gold because of that advice."],"from":"Denman’s version is in a letter he wrote in December 1869 to the Maryborough Mail. The Gympie Family History Society reprinted it on its website. Nash’s version is his own account, written in 1896 for the Gympie Times and reprinted in the Queenslander in 1917 under the title “How I discovered Gympie”.","known":["What both men agree on: They met at Denman’s timber camp near the Mary River in 1867, shortly before Nash found gold. Denman suggested a place to try.","Where they disagree: Denman wrote that he described a route to a waterhole, now Nash’s Gully, and that Nash was washing out gold within 24 hours. Nash wrote that Denman named Six Mile Creek as a likely place. Nash says he did not like the look of that creek and did not try it. He says he found his first gold further on, going down what is now Caledonian Hill.","Why it is doubtful: Denman wrote about two years after the event. Nash wrote nearly thirty years after it. Nash claimed a government reward for the discovery, so credit mattered. Denman said he could produce two witnesses who heard the first conversation. The sources found for this resource do not include statements from those witnesses. This resource does not decide who is right."],"src":["gfhsDenman","nash1917","fergDugdale"]},{"title":"The Curtis nugget: who found it, and a photograph nobody has found","where":"Gympie (then called Nashville) · February to April 1868","text":["In February 1868 miners at Gympie dug up a very large nugget of gold. It remains the largest nugget found in Queensland. About two months later it was melted down at the Sydney Mint. Many stories are told about it.","One story says George Curtis found it. Other stories say it was carried to the bank in a bag, in a sack or in a five gallon winch bucket. A later story says a second huge nugget, of 804 ounces, was found in the same field. Some people think a photograph was taken when Prince Alfred, who was visiting Australia, looked at the nugget in Sydney."],"from":"The main source is a 2020 paper by John Ferguson and Jim Dugdale in the Journal of Australasian Mining History. The authors collected newspaper reports, letters and family memories.","known":["What is documented: On 6 February 1868 Valentine Brigg, the nephew of claim holder George Curtis, dug up the nugget with a pick. Curtis wrote to the Maryborough Chronicle to say so. Weights reported in 1903, said to come from the Sydney Mint, give about 975 troy ounces before smelting and about 905 ounces of pure gold. The authors think these are the most reliable weights. Prince Alfred asked to handle the nugget at Government House in Sydney on 18 March 1868. His doctors stopped him because he had been shot six days earlier.","Why the rest is doubtful: The authors say that people who name Curtis as the finder are confusing owning the claim with finding the nugget. Reports of how it was carried differ. The authors say the second nugget of 804 ounces is an error, probably a transcription error or a mix-up between two creeks, which later writers repeated. They say it is highly probable that Lord Newry, an artist and photographer travelling with the Prince, photographed the nugget. But no picture of the nugget has been found. The Gympie and District Historical Society searched the British royal archives without success."],"src":["fergDugdale"]},{"title":"Where did the Mount Wheeler nuggets come from?","where":"Mount Wheeler, near Rockhampton · July 1869","text":["In July 1869 a boy named Cadden, aged twelve, found a huge nugget of gold at Mount Wheeler. The next day another boy, Luckman, found a second nugget close by. People wanted to know how so much gold came to be lying so near the surface in one small place.","Several explanations were told. One said the nuggets had been shot out through the cliffs of the mountain, or thrown out of a crater at its top. In 1924 a newspaper writer recorded more theories told by an old resident, Walter Neil. One was that a shipwrecked party had found the nuggets and left them because they could not carry them away."],"from":"The explanations come from newspaper reports of July 1869 and from a Morning Bulletin column of 1924. A compilation of these articles, taken from Trove, is on the aflaca.org.au website.","known":["What is documented: The Rockhampton Bulletin of 17 July 1869 reported the two nuggets. They weighed 258 ounces 11 pennyweights and 179 ounces 14 pennyweights. Reports of the time said the first was found about a foot below the surface. The Bulletin said the boy tried to lift it and could not. It said he “very coolly” said he would like to finish that piece of ground first.","Why the origin is doubtful: The Brisbane Courier of 26 July 1869 said no accepted theory could account for the nuggets, and that no quartz reef was visible above the spot. It mentioned the idea of a crater, but said the top of the mountain had not yet been climbed. It left the cause to Mr Daintree, who was about to survey the district. The Bulletin noted that small pieces of quartz stuck to the nuggets, which suggests they came from a reef. The sources found for this resource do not record a crater, a reef above the spot or a shipwreck. The origin is unproven."],"src":["aflacaMW","troveMW1869","troveMW26","troveMW1924"]},{"title":"The horse that kicked over a stone","where":"Byerstown, Palmer goldfield · 1876 to 1877","text":["A story from the Palmer goldfield says a miner went up a gully to look for his horses. One of the horses kicked over a large stone. Under the stone were many nuggets. The miner collected them in about three hours. Next day he asked the assistant warden to keep them in the warden’s safe. The warden weighed them. They came to 179 ounces and 3 pennyweights."],"from":"The story comes from William Hill, who took over from Warden Coward at Byerstown in April 1876. He told it in a reminiscence written about thirty years later. The text is reprinted on a family history website. The historian Noreen Kirkman describes Hill’s 1907 reminiscence “Forty-five Years’ Experiences in North Queensland” in her thesis on the Palmer goldfield.","known":["What is documented: Kirkman confirms that Hill served as assistant warden at Byerstown in 1876 and 1877. The story is told in his own reminiscence, about thirty years later.","Why it is doubtful: Noreen Kirkman, in her 1984 thesis on the Palmer goldfield, calls Hill’s 1907 reminiscence of his time at Byerstown “rollicking but not entirely trustworthy”. The web page that reprints the story does not say which of Hill’s writings it comes from, so this resource cannot be sure it is the same text. The miner is not named. No record of this find was found in the sources read for this resource. The story depends only on Hill’s word and is unproven."],"src":["hillPalmer","kirk84"]}]},"folkTeach":[{"q":"How could you test the story that a storm scattered the horses? Write down three kinds of evidence you would look for.","a":["Look for newspapers and official records of 1872. Look for what the prospectors said in their own words. Look for the earliest printed version of the storm story and its date.","A story that first appears many years after an event is weaker evidence than a report by someone who was there. On the council page, neither Hugh Mosman nor Jupiter mentions a storm in the words quoted.","Also discuss why Jupiter’s part was slow to be acknowledged. The Real lives page of this resource says it took about 30 years."]},{"q":"Denman wrote two years after the event. Nash wrote nearly thirty years after it. Does the time gap make one account more trustworthy? How could you test either story?","a":["The gap does not settle it. A shorter gap means memory is fresher. But a person who wants the credit may shape the story. Compare each account with documents made at the time, such as Nash’s reward claim and records of his first sales of gold.","Both accounts name other people who could have been asked. Ask students how tracing those people, or finding their statements, could help.","Ask students to separate what the two men agree on from what they do not. Agreement between two people who disagree on other points is useful evidence."]},{"q":"Why do people keep repeating the story of a second nugget when the authors of the 2020 paper say it is an error? How could you trace where a repeated claim began?","a":["The paper follows the 804 ounce claim through writers in 1889 and 1913. Each may have misread or repeated an earlier source. Students can practise following a claim back through footnotes to the earliest source.","Discuss the difference between a story with no evidence (a photograph that has not been found) and a story with evidence against it (a second nugget)."]},{"q":"Newspaper writers in 1869 offered several theories. Which theory could be tested today, and how?","a":["The reef theory could be tested by geologists. They could compare the gold in the nuggets with gold from nearby reefs. The Rockhampton Bulletin of 1869 already noted that bits of quartz on the nuggets suggested a reef.","The crater and shipwreck theories have no evidence in the sources. Ask students what evidence each would need. For a shipwreck it would be a wreck record or a report of a missing ship. For a crater it would be a geological survey of the mountain.","Ask why a writer in 1924 recalled theories from 55 years earlier, and how reliable that is."]},{"q":"Hill wrote his reminiscence about thirty years after he left the Palmer. What should a historian ask before using it as evidence?","a":["Ask who wrote it, when, why, and whether anyone else recorded the same event. Kirkman’s description suggests the reminiscence mixes real events with entertaining tales.","The warden’s records might show whether a parcel of 179 ounces was lodged. Kirkman lists the Byerstown warden’s letterbook among the records she used. Ask students how records like that could support or refute the story."]}]};
